<h1>Introduction to Visual Thinking</h1>

<p>
    Before diving into equations and algorithms, let's develop our intuition through <strong>visual thinking</strong>.
    The human brain processes images 60,000 times faster than text. By visualizing mathematical relationships,
    we can understand complex physics intuitively before formalizing our understanding.
</p>

<div class="info-box">
    <div class="info-box-title">Learning Objectives</div>
    <ul>
        <li>Learn to represent data and functions visually</li>
        <li>Understand function behavior through interactive exploration</li>
        <li>Build intuition before diving into mathematics</li>
        <li>Master basic plotting with Python</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<hr>

<h2>What is Visual Thinking?</h2>

<p>
    Visual thinking is the practice of using visual representations—graphs, charts, diagrams, and animations—to
    understand and communicate ideas. In computational physics, we use it to:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Explore</strong> - See patterns before proving them mathematically</li>
    <li><strong>Verify</strong> - Check if our calculations make sense</li>
    <li><strong>Communicate</strong> - Share insights with others effectively</li>
    <li><strong>Discover</strong> - Find unexpected behaviors in systems</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Plotting Basics: Your First Graphs</h2>

<p>
    Let's start with simple functions and build up. Every function tells a story—our job is to visualize that story.
</p>

<h3>Linear Functions</h3>

<p>
    The simplest non-trivial function: \( f(x) = 2x - 5 \)
</p>

<div class="interactive-container">
    <div class="interactive-title">Interactive: Linear Function Explorer</div>

    <div class="plot-container">
        <div id="linear-plot" style="width: 100%; height: 400px;"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="control-panel">
        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">
                Slope (m):
                <span class="control-label-value">2.00</span>
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="slider" id="slope-slider" min="-5" max="5" step="0.1" value="2">
        </div>

        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">
                Intercept (b):
                <span class="control-label-value">-5.00</span>
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="slider" id="intercept-slider" min="-10" max="10" step="0.5" value="-5">
        </div>

        <div class="btn-group">
            <button class="btn" onclick="resetLinear()">Reset</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="info-box">
    <div class="info-box-title">Key Insight</div>
    <p>
        The slope <code>m</code> controls how steep the line is. Positive slopes go up, negative slopes go down.
        The intercept <code>b</code> shifts the line up or down. Together, they completely determine the line!
    </p>
</div>

<h3>Quadratic Functions: The Power of Parabolas</h3>

<p>
    Quadratic functions appear everywhere in physics: projectile motion, springs, potential energy.
    The general form is \( f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \).
</p>

<div class="interactive-container">
    <div class="interactive-title">Interactive: Quadratic Function Explorer</div>

    <div class="plot-container">
        <div id="quadratic-plot" style="width: 100%; height: 400px;"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="control-panel">
        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">
                a (opens up/down):
                <span class="control-label-value">1.00</span>
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="slider" id="a-slider" min="-3" max="3" step="0.1" value="1">
        </div>

        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">
                b (shifts left/right):
                <span class="control-label-value">0.00</span>
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="slider" id="b-slider" min="-5" max="5" step="0.5" value="0">
        </div>

        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">
                c (shifts up/down):
                <span class="control-label-value">0.00</span>
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="slider" id="c-slider" min="-10" max="10" step="0.5" value="0">
        </div>

        <div class="btn-group" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            <button class="btn" onclick="resetQuadratic()">Reset</button>
            <div id="vertex-display" style="font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--neon-magenta); font-size: 0.9rem;">
                Vertex: (0.00, 0.00)
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="equation-display">
    <p><strong>Vertex Formula:</strong></p>
    $$ x_{vertex} = -\frac{b}{2a}, \quad y_{vertex} = f(x_{vertex}) $$
</div>

<hr>

<h2>Comparing Function Growth</h2>

<p>
    Different functions grow at different rates. Let's compare how \( \sqrt{x} \), \( x \), \( x^2 \), and \( x^3 \)
    behave near the origin and far away.
</p>

<div class="interactive-container">
    <div class="interactive-title">Interactive: Function Growth Comparison</div>

    <div class="plot-container">
        <div id="growth-plot" style="width: 100%; height: 400px;"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="control-panel">
        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">
                X Range (0 to):
                <span class="control-label-value">5.00</span>
            </label>
            <input type="range" class="slider" id="range-slider" min="1" max="10" step="0.1" value="5">
        </div>

        <div class="btn-group">
            <button class="btn" onclick="updateGrowthPlot()">Update Plot</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="warning-box">
    <div class="info-box-title">Important Observation</div>
    <p>
        Near \( x = 0 \), higher powers grow <em>slower</em> (they're flatter).
        Far from the origin, higher powers completely dominate. This asymptotic behavior is crucial in physics!
    </p>
</div>

<hr>

<h2>Python Code Examples</h2>

<p>
    Let's see how to create these plots in Python using <strong>Plotly</strong> — the same library powering
    the interactive plots you see on this page. Learning one plotting approach works everywhere!
</p>

<div class="info-box">
    <div class="info-box-title">One Library, Less Confusion</div>
    <p>
        Throughout this book, we use <strong>only Plotly</strong> for all visualizations. The same code patterns
        you see on this webpage work in your Python scripts. No need to learn multiple plotting libraries —
        focus on physics and problem-solving instead!
    </p>
</div>

<div class="tabs">
    <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-button active">Linear Function</button>
        <button class="tab-button">Quadratic Function</button>
        <button class="tab-button">Multiple Functions</button>
        <button class="tab-button">Prompt</button>
    </div>

    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="info-box">
            <div class="info-box-title">How to Prompt an LLM</div>
            <p>
                Instead of memorizing syntax, learn to <strong>describe what you want</strong>.
                Here are effective prompts you could use with ChatGPT, Claude, or other AI assistants
                to generate the code shown in the other tabs.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block-container">
            <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-lang">Prompt for Linear Function</span>
                <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-text">Create a Python script using Plotly to plot the linear function f(x) = 2x - 5
from x = -10 to 10. Use a dark theme with cyan-colored line. Set fixed axis
ranges: x from -10 to 10, y from -35 to 35. Make axes gray (#808080) to
differentiate from the data curve.</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block-container">
            <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-lang">Prompt for Quadratic Function</span>
                <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-text">Write a Python function using Plotly that plots a quadratic function
f(x) = ax² + bx + c. The function should calculate and highlight the
minimum or maximum point with a magenta marker. Use dark theme, cyan line
for the parabola, gray axes. Set x range to [-10, 10] and y range to
[-25, 50].</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block-container">
            <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-lang">Prompt for Multiple Functions</span>
                <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-text">Create a Plotly visualization comparing four functions: √x, x, x², and x³
on the same plot from x = 0.1 to 5. Use different neon colors (cyan, magenta,
yellow, lime) for each function. Apply dark theme and show a legend. This
should demonstrate how different power functions grow at different rates.</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="warning-box">
            <div class="info-box-title">Pro Tips for Effective Prompts</div>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Be specific</strong>: Mention the library (Plotly), colors, ranges</li>
                <li><strong>Describe the goal</strong>: What should the visualization show?</li>
                <li><strong>Include constraints</strong>: Fixed ranges, specific styling</li>
                <li><strong>Iterate</strong>: If the first result isn't perfect, ask for adjustments</li>
                <li><strong>Learn from output</strong>: Study the generated code to understand patterns</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tab-content active">
        <div class="code-block-container">
            <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-lang">Python</span>
                <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-python">import numpy as np
import plotly.graph_objects as go

# Generate x values
x = np.linspace(-10, 10, 200)

# Define the function
y = 2*x - 5

# Create the plot
fig = go.Figure()
fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
    x=x, y=y,
    mode='lines',
    name='f(x) = 2x - 5',
    line=dict(color='cyan', width=2)
))

# Styling
fig.update_layout(
    template='plotly_dark',
    title='Linear Function',
    xaxis_title='x',
    yaxis_title='f(x)',
    xaxis=dict(range=[-10, 10], zeroline=True),
    yaxis=dict(range=[-35, 35], zeroline=True)
)

fig.show()</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="code-block-container">
            <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-lang">Python</span>
                <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-python">import numpy as np
import plotly.graph_objects as go

# Quadratic function plotter
def plot_quadratic(a=1, b=0, c=0):
    x = np.linspace(-10, 10, 200)
    y = a*x**2 + b*x + c

    fig = go.Figure()

    # Plot the parabola
    fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
        x=x, y=y,
        mode='lines',
        name=f'f(x) = {a}x² + {b}x + {c}',
        line=dict(color='cyan', width=2)
    ))

    # Calculate and plot the minimum/maximum
    if a != 0:
        x_vertex = -b / (2*a)
        y_vertex = a*x_vertex**2 + b*x_vertex + c

        fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
            x=[x_vertex], y=[y_vertex],
            mode='markers',
            name='Minimum' if a > 0 else 'Maximum',
            marker=dict(color='magenta', size=10)
        ))

    # Styling
    fig.update_layout(
        template='plotly_dark',
        title='Quadratic Function',
        xaxis_title='x',
        yaxis_title='f(x)',
        xaxis=dict(range=[-10, 10]),
        yaxis=dict(range=[-25, 50])
    )

    return fig

# Try different values
fig = plot_quadratic(a=1, b=0, c=0)
fig.show()</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="code-block-container">
            <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-lang">Python</span>
                <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-python">import numpy as np
import plotly.graph_objects as go

# Generate x values
x = np.linspace(0.1, 5, 200)

# Create figure
fig = go.Figure()

# Add multiple functions
functions = [
    {'name': '√x', 'y': np.sqrt(x), 'color': 'cyan'},
    {'name': 'x', 'y': x, 'color': 'magenta'},
    {'name': 'x²', 'y': x**2, 'color': 'yellow'},
    {'name': 'x³', 'y': x**3, 'color': 'lime'}
]

for func in functions:
    fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
        x=x, y=func['y'],
        mode='lines',
        name=func['name'],
        line=dict(color=func['color'], width=2)
    ))

# Styling
fig.update_layout(
    template='plotly_dark',
    title='Function Growth Comparison',
    xaxis_title='x',
    yaxis_title='f(x)',
    showlegend=True
)

fig.show()</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

